@justjoshinyou13 Right, it's underappreciated how many of our existing success measures break down when you start to scale up to really ambitious tasks with a ton of agents working concurrently.

We have a decent sense for how good singular agents are at some things, but as you say the problem with swarms is that you have to worry about cascading failures and other weird social dynamics. Arguably pass^n measures are even more important there, since more agents means more sources of inconsistency across runs.

Implies that lot of benchmark extrapolation is "not even wrong."

2124200938751623568.text:
@tszzl @thinkingshivers Better assessment is that AI has succeeded dramatically in 

1) verifiable domains with rapid / cheap / digital learning loops;

2) unverifiable domains with rapid / cheap / digital learning loops AND forgiving, highly disjunctive success conditions (many, many ways to make a halfway-decent music video).

The long road ahead is concentrated in a a mix of tasks with expensive, slow, supra-digital learning loops (incl. some verifiable ones) and success conditions that are simultaneously strict, ill-defined, and subject to rapid change (the last one is generally underestimated; consider how the meaning of "good" research or "good" fiction changes year-to-year).

To see why the nature of the learning loops matters as much, if not more than the verifiability of the success / reward condition, consider that "amount of dollars in bank account" and "amount of years lived" are both verifiable, and then meditate on what it would take to design an RL loop for learning to maximize either.

